			},
			"offsets": {
				"from": 167920,
				"to": 172080
			},
			"text": " Without an index, you will do up to a million check in the worst case scenario."
		},
		{
			"timestamps": {
				"from": "00:02:52,080",
				"to": "00:02:54,680"
			},
			"offsets": {
				"from": 172080,
				"to": 174680
			},
			"text": " Without an index, you will do way less than that."
		},
		{
			"timestamps": {
				"from": "00:02:54,680",
				"to": "00:02:58,800"
			},
			"offsets": {
				"from": 174680,
				"to": 178800
			},
			"text": " Most databases store their indexes in a structure called a B3."
		},
		{
			"timestamps": {
				"from": "00:02:58,800",
				"to": "00:03:03,000"
			},
			"offsets": {
				"from": 178800,
				"to": 183000
			},
			"text": " And a B3 works the way that the example from the article showed."
		},
		{
			"timestamps": {
				"from": "00:03:03,000",
